From 9a8340efdad3571063720dc422c91f3b79c426b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] perf(dsv41): align 64-row Q-B decode tiles with 128x128
 tile-contiguous weights

Base: 1107 vLLM patch head 2756478b60b90700b9918b457aeb38657da637b5. Replaces
candidate-47's 0768 (PR #1016 as submitted). It touches only gemm.py and its
test, which no other patch of the 0762..0769 series changes.

Rationale:
Q-B (wq_b) at TP4/TP5 (8192 x 1280) and TP3 (12288 x 1280) keeps its FP8 weight
in 128x128 tile-contiguous slabs (pack_weight_tiles, 16 KiB per tile). 1107's
64-row decode tiles for both shapes use block_N = 64, so two CTAs share each
slab, each reading an 8 KiB half per K step. The grid doubles to 128 / 192 CTAs
on 48 SMs, and every CTA repeats the A and SFA loads. With block_N = 128,
block_K = 128 and num_stages = 2 (1107's 12288 tile already uses 2 stages), each
CTA reads whole slabs, and the 64 / 96 CTAs still cover the 48 SMs.

Only the 64-row Q-B tiles change. The submitted revision also widened the
32-row Q-B tiles and the 16-row (4096, 1280) tile, dropped threads=64 from the
64-row (4096, 1280) tile, and registered (16384|6144|2048, 1280). This revision
keeps 1107's settings for all of those:
- In the model, the (4096, 1280) decode tiles serve only TP8's Q-B. The indexer
  Q-B runs decode_rope_mxfp4, which linear.py builds with the kernel defaults
  (block_M 16, block_N 128, 2 stages, 256 threads). It never reads
  DECODE_FP8_CONFIGS. On the TP8 Q-B, dropping threads=64 does not speed up the
  64-row tile (M=48: +0.2 us warm, +1.1 us cold, mean of five processes). The
  16-row block_N = 128 tile is faster at M <= 6 but slower at M = 12 (+0.4 us
  warm in every process).
- The chained bench builds its indexer kernel from the (4096, 1280) tiles. On
  that kernel, block_N = 128 at 128 threads halves the rows of each serial
  RoPE/MXFP4 epilogue pass (2 * threads / block_N rows). M = 12 therefore needs
  six dependent passes instead of three, which is the bench's M = 12 slowdown.
- The 32-row tiles gained nothing at M = 24 beyond the interval. Single
  processes put the TP3 pair 4..6 us slower with the weights in L2.
- The TP2/TP6/TP7 shapes are outside the chained Q-B bench. They need their own
  change and their own measurement: TP6/TP7's trimmed rank unpacks and repacks
  Q-B.

Quality:
block_N and num_stages only change which CTA computes which output columns and
how the loads are pipelined. Each output accumulates K in the same order, so
decode outputs are bit-identical (torch.equal) to 1107's tiles, to row-major
decode and to prefill.

Tests:
- pytest tests/kernels/quantization/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_linear.py
  tests/kernels/quantization/test_deepseek_v41_block_scale_words.py
  tests/models/test_deepseek_v4_1_attention.py
  tests/models/test_deepseek_v4_1_l2_schedule.py
  tests/kernels/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_pdl.py on candidate-47 with this
  patch: 187 passed on each of 2 boots (sp4), including
  test_tile_contiguous_fp8_matches_row_major.
- Chained Q-B + indexer Q-B (CUDA-graph replay; warm, cold and NEXT L2;
  M = 1..48). Same-session ABBA on sp4 against candidate-47 without #1016
  (1107's gemm.py), with 1107's table timed in each process. The M = 1..24
  kernels are identical to 1107's. At M = 48 with the weights in L2: TP4
  -3.1 us, TP3 -5.1 us paired (cross-image -2.9 / -7.7 us; with the indexer
  kernel as the model builds it, -3.0 / -3.9 us). Cold and NEXT are unchanged
  within the interval. No cell is slower. Outputs are bit-identical across
  images.

Upstream status: local DeepSeek-V4.1 SM12x TileLang decode tile table; not for
upstream vLLM.
---
 .../quantization/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_linear.py       | 2 ++
 vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/tilelang/gemm.py                  | 6 ++++--
 2 files changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tests/kernels/quantization/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_linear.py b/tests/kernels/quantization/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_linear.py
index b5c16fb..0944678 100644
--- a/tests/kernels/quantization/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_linear.py
+++ b/tests/kernels/quantization/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_linear.py
@@ -602,6 +602,8 @@ def test_tile_contiguous_fp8_matches_row_major(default_vllm_config, n, k):
 
     for block_M in DECODE_TILE_ROWS:
         cfg = fp8_decode_config(n, k, block_M)
+        if block_M == DECODE_ROWS and n >= 8192:
+            assert (cfg["block_N"], cfg["block_K"]) == (128, 128), (n, block_M, cfg)
         k_ref = mxfp8_gemm_decode(n, k, **cfg, padded_rows=True)
         k_tiled = mxfp8_gemm_decode(
             n, k, **cfg, padded_rows=True, tile_contiguous=True
diff --git a/vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/tilelang/gemm.py b/vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/tilelang/gemm.py
index c2443ad..af0248f 100644
--- a/vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/tilelang/gemm.py
+++ b/vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/tilelang/gemm.py
@@ -2499,15 +2499,17 @@ TILE_CONTIGUOUS_FP8: set[tuple[int, int]] = {
 
 DECODE_FP8_CONFIGS: dict[tuple[int, ...], dict[int, dict]] = {
     # Attention: Q-B (TP3, TP4..7, TP8 / indexer Q-B) and fused Q-A/KV.
+    # 64-row Q-B tiles read whole 128-row tile-contiguous slabs (block_N =
+    # TILE_WEIGHT_N); N // 128 >= 64 tiles still cover the 48 SMs.
     (12288, 1280): {
         16: dict(block_N=128, block_K=128, num_stages=3),
         32: dict(block_N=64, block_K=128, num_stages=3),
-        64: dict(block_N=64, block_K=128, num_stages=2),
+        64: dict(block_N=128, block_K=128, num_stages=2),
     },
     (8192, 1280): {
         16: dict(block_N=128, block_K=128, num_stages=3),
         32: dict(block_N=64, block_K=128, num_stages=3),
-        64: dict(block_N=64, block_K=128, num_stages=3),
+        64: dict(block_N=128, block_K=128, num_stages=2),
     },
     (4096, 1280): {
         16: dict(block_N=64, block_K=128, num_stages=3),
